  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The maximum number of electrons that could be present in a shell is given by the rule 2n<sup>2</sup>.</p><p>Where n is the shell number.</p><p>The first shell K shell can accommodate a maximum of 2 electrons&nbsp;(<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></math>).</p><p>The second shell L shell&nbsp;can accommodate a maximum of 8 electrons (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>8</mn></math>).</p><p>Likewise, the 4th shell named N can accommodate a maximum of 32 electrons (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>32</mn></math>).</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
